Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A conductor, due to the presence of free electrons, allow for electric charge/current to flow through the material with ease. An insulator does not allow electric charge/current to easily flow through the material, as the electrons in such a material are tightly bound to the atoms.</p><h4>Significance</h4><h4>Induction</h4><p>Since electrons are able to easily move in a conductive material, it can generate strong inductive effects. On the other hand, insulators can only generate local polar effects.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a conductor, the charged particles remain on the edges of the object\">In a conductor, the charged particles remain on the edges of the object</a><ul><li>The equilibrium state that free-flowing charge settles into.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Conductivity is determined by the resistance of a material\">Conductivity is determined by the resistance of a material</a><ul><li>Quantifies “freely” — conduction is a matter of degree, not a binary.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Electrostatic induction polarizes a neutral conductor\">Electrostatic induction polarizes a neutral conductor</a><ul><li>Induction is what causes charge to flow through a neutral conductor.</li></ul></li></ul>","A good person is someone who has an above average moral character":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that someone who is “good” goes above and beyond the standard moral conventions that agents would rationally agree to in the original position.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the definition that Rawls gives, and the definition which he uses in his book.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>A good person engages in good acts. A good act is an non-compulsed act which is intended to advance the rational long-term plan of another person.</p><h4>References</h4>","A limited war is best fought on the strategic defensive and the operational offensive":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>As a part of his belief in limited warfare, Julian Corbett argued for militaries to adopt a strategically defensive, but operationally offensive approach towards engaging the enemy as to shift the cost-benefit calculus of the enemy until it is more cost efficient for them to surrender.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the core concepts of Corbettian strategy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This does not mean that a military should adopt such a stance, <strong>always</strong>. During the opening stages of the war, it may be beneficial to go in the strategic offensive and engage the enemy while they are still unprepared. After establishing control in enemy, the military may then switch to a strategically defensive posture to slowly weaken the enemy.</p><h4>References</h4>","An unrealized gains tax may force owners to liquidate illiquid assets just to pay taxes":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An unrealized gains tax could force people to sell of assets just in order to pay their tax, and in cases where the asset is illiquid, this could lead to a distortion of the actual value of the asset, meaning that in the long run, investment may be weakened. Additionally, if an asset is overvalued, it may be overtaxed, and if undervalued, undertaxed. Having to valuate the asset also introduces transactions costs.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most significant arguments against an unrealized gains tax, as it would distort the financial markets and cause a decrease in investment.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property facilitates trade\">Private property facilitates trade</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The profit motive sharpens incentives\">The profit motive sharpens incentives</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation\">Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation</a></li></ul>","Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The principle of careers open to talents does not do enough to rectify the preexistent inequalities in the world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Rawls uses this suggest the necessity of a difference principle, namely the principle of fair equality of opportunity.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Rawls argues that careers open to talents does not mitigate either social contingencies or natural contingencies. Therefore, we should not adopt it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies\">The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies</a><ul><li>The same argumentative move applied to a different principle. Both failures push Rawls toward the difference principle.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy\">Meritocracy</a><ul><li>A main critique of meritocracy is that it is unable to account for the social or natural contingencies that arise, which is captured in this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Clarity comes in grades, from familiarity, to abstract definition, to practical consequence":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Understanding an idea admits of degrees: one may recognise the term when it appears, one may be able to state a definition of it, and one may know what difference it would make in experience if it were true, and only the third counts as full clarity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This gives a test for whether a distinction is real, since two ideas at the third grade of clarity are the same idea if they imply the same practical consequences.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The grades are cumulative and the second is the deceptive one, because the ability to define a term convincingly resembles understanding closely enough to be mistaken for it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A distinction in meaning is only extant given some actual change in the way we act\">A distinction in meaning is only extant given some actual change in the way we act</a><ul><li>The criterion that the third grade supplies.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We often mistake our own unclearness of thought for some inherent mystery in the subject of our thought\">We often mistake our own unclearness of thought for some inherent mystery in the subject of our thought</a><ul><li>The characteristic error of someone stalled at the second grade.</li></ul></li></ul>","Collectivist countries are more lonely less individualist ones":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is evidence to suggest that, contrary to popular belief, collectivist states have a higher level of loneliness than individualist ones.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This challenges something that is commonly believed regarding collectivist/individualist countries.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>  There seems to be a relatively strong correlation between the collectivity of a state and the level of loneliness in that state. This seems to be because collectivist states are more tribal and people typically don’t associate outside of their “tribe.”</p><h4>References</h4>","Conductivity is determined by the resistance of a material":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The more resistance a material, the less conductive it is, and the more thermal radiation is released as waste.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Different conductivities can change the speed at which a charge moves through the material. This is significant for many physics problems.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not\">A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not</a><ul><li>Resistance is what makes “freely” a spectrum rather than a binary.</li></ul></li></ul>","Control of the Eurasian heartland dictates control of the world":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Mackinder’s Heartland Thesis argued that whoever controlled the heartland (see below, defined as the “Pivot Area”) would be able to control the Rimlands (defined below as the “Inner or marginal crescent”), and thereby, they would be able to control the entire world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a significant theory from international relations and realpolitik that informed many later theories regarding geopolitics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p> I think that the theory carries some weight, though some historical evidence suggests that the state that controls the Pivot Area is not guaranteed to be able to control the entire world. I think that this weakens the theory, since a theory could really be produced about any region of the world, with the result that specific region has some specific trait that allows it to conquer the world. That being said, there is other historical evidence to support the theory, including many of the nomadic conquerors. It seems that the most important factor in determining the effectiveness of the conquests of the state in the pivot area is its access to transportation technology that is advanced and flexible. In that way, this theory could be considered “technological” more than “geographical.”</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Control of the Eurasian rimland dictates control of the world\">Control of the Eurasian rimland dictates control of the world</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take\">Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence\">The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence</a></li></ul>","Despite some civilizational convergence, there is no universality in culture":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Although some aspects of the world have become more similar, on the whole, civilizational differences still define us more than similarities.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This refutes the idea that there is some universal cultural element to humanity.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>The spread of Western consumption patterns and pop culture does not relate to the creation of a universal civilization.<ul><li>The spread of Western media does not either:<ul><li>There is no data for how widespread it actually is.</li><li>Different cultures could interpret the same Western media in different ways.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Language has also not promoted the creation of a universal culture.<ul><li>English facilitates <em>inter-cultural</em> exchange, but this does not means that there is a universal civilization.</li><li>The frequency of a language’s use is also tied to the relative ability of the mother nation to exert control, and the West (specifically the US)’s control of this is slipping.</li></ul></li><li>Greater commercialization and trade across cultures does not necessarily mean the creation of a universal civilization.<ul><li>Economic interdependence could either by peace-inducing or war-inducing.</li></ul></li><li>Modern civilization could be more similar to each other than traditional ones, however:<ul><li>There is shared technology and fast exchange of ideas, information, and trends.</li><li>It is industrial, rather than agricultural, meaning that geography plays less of a role in determining the way a state is (and thus causing differences between states).</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies\">Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society\">Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society</a><ul><li>This idea somewhat clashes with the current note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state\">Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state</a><ul><li>Specific example of the current note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The IMF and World Bank have been criticized for promoting Western neocolonial interests\">The IMF and World Bank have been criticized for promoting Western neocolonial interests</a><ul><li>This is an example of this note in practice.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Civilization is the process by which time preferences diminish\">Civilization is the process by which time preferences diminish</a><ul><li>But this seems to be something that happens universally has civilizations become more advanaced?</li></ul></li></ul>","Dirigisme describes a focus on internal management; Neomercantilism describes a focus on external management":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Dirigisme describes a state that manages internal economic affairs, whereas Neomercantilism describes a state that maintains always a current account surplus through government intervention.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The terms are similar in that both describe a state’s control of the economy, but one is specifically referring the management of the economy as an internal measure, and the other describes the management of an economy as the management of some external feature.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Neo-mercantilism is the modern adaptation of historical mercantilism\">Neo-mercantilism is the modern adaptation of historical mercantilism</a></li></ul>","Double membrane, circular chromosome, bacteria-like ribosomes, and independent replication are evidence for endosymbiosis":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If an organelle has a double membrane, circular chromosome (like a bacteria), bacteria-like ribosomes, and independent replication through binary fission, they are most likely an organelle that was endocytosed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These four pieces of evidence can indicate whether an organelle was originally independent and became an organelle after being endocytosed.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Some cell organelles are related to bacteria\">Some cell organelles are related to bacteria</a></li></ul>","Economic liberalization is positively correlated with democratization":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Economic liberalization is often a cause of democratization in many countries, a fact that is proven through empirical evidence.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents a third view that regards the relationship between capitalism and democracy, and presents an empirical evidence in support of this view.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are at least two main observations that could explain this correlation:</p><ol><li>Capitalist policies promote growth, allowing for newly accrued economic benefits to be distributed to all people in the system. Redistribution does not produce growth.</li><li>Democratic socialism collapses into tyranny, and thus cannot exist. Economic centralization provides too much power to those in power; “power corrupts and absolute power corrupts absolutely.”</li></ol><p>Some argue that countries such as the UAE, Hong Kong, or Singapore stand as proof that economic liberalization does not indicate a growth in democracy, though I would argue that these nations are not truly capitalist, incorporating far more dirigisme and neo-mercantilist elements.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a><ul><li>This seems to refute the claims made in this note, since it seems that while some of these countries have become democratic, or where democratic when such policies were implemented, some have not become democratic.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a><ul><li>Perhaps the fundamental nature of the society plays a larger role. Despite the economic liberalization in China, it has yet to be politically liberalized.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom\">Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom</a><ul><li>This seems to be the underlying principle of the current note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy\">Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy</a><ul><li>The claims in the current note seems to align with the idea here, since economic liberalization is something recent, and it does seem to correlate with the rise of democracy in the West.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property facilitates trade\">Private property facilitates trade</a><ul><li>For liberalization to occur, private property is required.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization\">Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization</a><ul><li>Huntington would argue that the ideas represented in this note are wrong.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding\">Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding</a><ul><li>So maybe this isn’t the case?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism undermines democracy\">Capitalism undermines democracy</a><ul><li>Related to the above.</li></ul></li></ul>","Fulfillment can be found within history":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The standard Christian reading of history, such as the one that is espoused by St. Augustine, argues that history is but an interim period for humans after the death of Christ and his second coming. Joachim of Floris, on the other hand, argues that fulfillment can be found within history, specifically, through the third dispensation, i.e., the transformation of the world into a more spiritual one.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This view was later adopted by the Franciscans and was used against the existence of the church (who would play no part in this more spiritual world where every one was in tune with religion).</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Ultimately, this theory would influence the creation of secular theories of history in the future, such as the ones of Proudhon or Marx. This is because it moved the end goal of history from an extra-historical dimension within history itself, which was easily adopted by other secular conceptions of history.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 155</p><p>The Christian doctrine from Augustine to Thomas had mastered history theologically by excluding the temporal relevance of the last things. This exclusion was achieved by the transposition of the original expectations into a realm beyond historical existence. <strong>Joachim viewed everything in a historical perspective.</strong></p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity\">Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity</a><ul><li>This was one of the ideas that influenced Marx, though he may not have known it.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The final stage of history is a positive one\">The final stage of history is a positive one</a><ul><li>That a final stage of history can even exist on the Earth is something that is borrowed from Joachim’s conception of history.</li></ul></li></ul>","Having a large middle class is beneficial for a society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Having a middle class is supposed to have many benefits for society, including ensuring both political and economic stability and reducing class conflict.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common idea, first espoused by Aristotle.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle argues that having a large middle class allows a city to better achieve its <em>telos</em>, since there is less factional conflict (as there would be between a clear upper and lower class).</p><ul><li>The middle class align with his doctine of the mean, since they are not subject to the often times degrading influence of being a member of the upper class, and also not the struggles of the lower class.</li><li>The middle class is most likely to follow reason.</li><li>The upper class is too haughty and the lower class too slavish. Therefore the middle class are then the best citizens and are best able to contribute to the city.</li></ul><p>In a polity is where a large middle class is most likely to emerge, and this is also where political participation (citizenship) is extended to the most amount of people.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>In a way, having many middle class people is the political manifestation of the golden mean.</li></ul></li></ul>","Historical judgment is relative":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Historical judgement is necessarily something that is relative and dependent on the perspective from which you look at a topic.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This should impel a sense of epistemic humility when we both cast judgment on the past or just when we make general claims about events that occurred in the past.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Orosius used this to support his argument regarding the importance and omnipresence of suffering in the world. For instance, even when the Romans civilized Europe, the barbarian tribes were harmed.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 179</p><p>Thus when pagans say that Rome happy in the times of her continuous triumphs, victories, imposing processions, and wealth, one has to point out to them that whenever Rome conquered and was happy the rest of the world was conquered and unhappy.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As history progresses, morality advances as well\">As history progresses, morality advances as well</a><ul><li>Even just accepting the trivially true premise that morality changes across time, this is something that we should look out for.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The history of monasticism is a continual struggle with the problem of secularizing the influence of wealth\">The history of monasticism is a continual struggle with the problem of secularizing the influence of wealth</a><ul><li>This, for example, could be example of a perspective on history that is tainted by modern perspectives.</li></ul></li></ul>","International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the high cost of running a patent office and ensuring that all inventions are account for, this may result in a drag on the economies of developing nations (the ones that don’t yet have technology). As a result, this system only serves to benefit countries that are already hubs of innovation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This fits into Chang’s general argument regarding the weakening of existing patent and IPR laws.</p><h4>Quote</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Martin Wolf, <em>Why Globalization Works</em>, pg. 217</p><p>[Patents and similar forms of protection] is a rent-extraction device for [developing nations], with potentially devastating consequences for their ability to educate their people (because of copyright), adapt deigns for their own use (ditto) and deal with severe challenges of public health.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs\">Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs</a><ul><li>The individual-level behaviour that produces the incumbent advantage described here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation\">Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research\">Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research</a></li></ul>","Japanese companies are fundamentally structured to favor incremental progress over drastic breakthroughs":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Japanese companies care fundamentally structured differently than Western ones, making them better at incremental progress than drastic breakthroughs.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why Japanese companies have dominated some fields while Western companies dominate over ones.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Overall, the Japanese system is more decentralized and shareholder focused. For instance,</p><ul><li>unions are company based, and not industry based.</li><li>workers are more generalized than in Western firms, so they can solve problems all along a production process.<ul><li>The <em>andon</em> cord is something unique to Japanese manufacturing, whereby anyone alone a production line can pull the string if they spot any flaws in the product.</li></ul></li><li>there is very little equity from investors, and its mostly from other companies. This means that companies are not in pursuit of short-term profit, but can rather focus on long-term goals.</li><li>people who typically work their whole lives at a single company, and there is never any mass layoffs.</li><li>promotions based on seniority, not productivity.</li></ul><p>All of these facets of the system are interconnected and present as a unified system whereby they cannot be implemented individually. And all of this means that the Japanese firm is able to expand into a variety of industries that range widely.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When there is innovation, incumbents in a market are at a disadvantage\">When there is innovation, incumbents in a market are at a disadvantage</a><ul><li>The general mechanism this corporate structure runs into.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"IBM failed to invest in emerging technologies, leading to the declining market share in many industries\">IBM failed to invest in emerging technologies, leading to the declining market share in many industries</a><ul><li>A Western case of the same outcome, arrived at through decisions rather than through structure.</li></ul></li></ul>","Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is interesting because it is in contrast with our putative morality today, which makes morality egalitarian. On the other hand, Aristotle claims that this virtue is not achievable by all.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>The excessive vice of magnificence is vulgarity, meaning spending too much in a tasteless fashion. The deficient vice of magnificence is pettiness, meaning spending too little, and when spending, spending in a tasteless fashion. Since it requires having great wealth to be magnificent, this is a virtue restricted to those who have the means to acquire great wealth.</p><h4>References</h4>","Only when moribund do we have a clear perspective on our own life":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that death is somehow profound and brings about an evaluation of the way that we live which is deeper than would otherwise have been able to be achieved.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This seems to be something that is suggested by many works of literature, and makes us aware of the fact that when we are not about to die, we may not have the fullest perspective on life.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>This idea has been referenced in many works of literature, including:</p><ul><li><em>The Snows of Kilimanjaro</em>: where Harry gains a deeper understanding of the “sins” during his life during his death.</li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"East of Eden\">East of Eden</a>: when Adam was on his deathbed, he was finally able to understand <em>timshel</em>, the experiences of his life, and the experience of his sons.</li></ul><h4>Note</h4><p>The claim needs a qualification. Clarity about a wasted life is a cognitive achievement and not a moral one, so the deathbed recognition does not by itself discharge what preceded it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><em>Youth</em><ul><li>I think that this tale presents a complementary message about the nature of youth, and how our explorations during youth have some greater meaning, despite the struggles that we might face.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Recognizing one’s failures late does not by itself constitute redemption\">Recognizing one’s failures late does not by itself constitute redemption</a><ul><li>The qualification stated as its own claim.</li></ul></li></ul>","Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Most systems, when there are internal imbalances, will tend towards equilibrium due to some equalizing force within the system.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This seems to be an inductive rule regard the many systems that exist across many fields, and could be used as a conceptual framework to predict the outcomes of yet-to-exist systems.</p><h4>Explanation/Examples</h4><p>This is something that is observable throughout all different fields, namely the principle that when an external force attempts to impose some change on a system, the system will internally readjust such that the system returns to its original state. For instance, this is seen through:</p><ul><li>Le Chatelier's Principle</li><li>Political Realism attempts to create a balance of power that limits the power of an individual state to maintain equilibrium<ul><li>An example of this principle in practice in international relations.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Counterexamples</h4><ul><li>When silver was introduced en mass in the Spanish Empire in the 16th century, hyperinflation and economic collapse ensued.<ul><li>An example of how a system without limitations eventually collapsed.</li></ul></li><li>The one-child policy made irreversible changes to China's population dynamics.</li></ul><h4>Notes</h4><p>Perhaps the reason that some systems don't actually settle into a stable equilibrium is because not all systems have a stable equilibrium. Some systems may be have unstable equilibriums whereby once the system is disrupted, the system collapses to a completely different state.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors\">Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors</a><ul><li>An example of this from political science.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When two conductors touch, they both acquire the same charge such that the overall charge is preserved\">When two conductors touch, they both acquire the same charge such that the overall charge is preserved</a><ul><li>An example from eletromagnetism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Once the threshold potential is reached, voltage-gated sodium ion channels open, and depolarization begins\">Once the threshold potential is reached, voltage-gated sodium ion channels open, and depolarization begins</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Consequence Argument holds that acting otherwise would require altering the laws of nature or the past":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If determinism is true, then acting otherwise at a moment of decision would require either the laws of nature or the prior state of the world to have been different, and since neither is within anyone’s power, no one is ever able to do otherwise.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the strongest incompatibilist argument, and it is what a categorical analysis of ability has to answer.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Lewis replies by distinguishing two theses. On the weak thesis, an agent is able to do something such that, were they to do it, a law would have been broken; on the strong thesis, the agent is able to do something that would constitute or cause a law being broken. Only the strong thesis is incredible, and the compatibilist needs only the weak one. Van Inwagen objects that the weak thesis is too weak to be worth having, since it lets one affirm both that an agent is able to act and that a necessary condition of that action fails to obtain.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Determinism and free will are incompatible\">Determinism and free will are incompatible</a><ul><li>The conclusion this argument supports.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The conditional analysis of ability defines free action rather than free will\">The conditional analysis of ability defines free action rather than free will</a><ul><li>The weaker analysis this argument was designed to bypass.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exam represents a meritocracy, as it allows people to achieve high ranking government positions through hard work, rather than simply through nepotism. This sets China apart from many other civilizations of the world in a similar time period.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the highly efficient Imperial Chinese state institutions, as the Civil Service Exams ensured that only the most qualified applicants are able to make it into government.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>Its meritocratic nature was more apparently early in its existence, since by the Qing, it was certainly the case that the examine was no longer meritocratic to the same extent that it was earlier.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a><ul><li>This could partly be sustained by the meritocracy.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions\">The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions</a><ul><li>They were formed through meritocracy.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state\">The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state</a><ul><li>This prevents another perspective on the issue.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Taiping Rebellion failed due to internal instability":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The main reason for the failure of the Taiping Rebellion was the Tianjing Incident whereby most of the effective Taiping leadership was either killed, or fled. This doomed the state.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>A competing claim could be made that the Taiping rebellion was destroyed due to pressure from the Qing, though this note argues that the larger factor was an internal one.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Due to the growing power of Yang Xiuqing, the East King, Hong decided to order Wei Changhui, the North King to come in and kill Yang and his supporters. But then, Wei also attempted to seize power, though Shi was able to counter him. In the end, this episode merely weakened the Taiping leadership.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Successful rebellions need a constructive element\">Successful rebellions need a constructive element</a><ul><li>This note described what ultimately lead to the downfall of the Taiping, not their lack of a constructive element.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Three Represents marked a major ideological shift for the CCP":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Three Represents (三个代表) marked a shift of party ideology from class struggle to economic development, helping the party align with the rapid economic development that has occurred in China in the past decade.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most important chances to party ideology, since it marked a major shift from the ideology of previous Chinese leaders.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>By allowing the wealthy elite into the party, the CCP successfully integrated the economic elite into the party system by arguing that entrepreneurs were essential to “advanced productive forces.” This was done partly to ensure the survival of the party, by essentially co-opting the emerging economic elites and intellectuals, ensuring that these powerful people were on their side.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China&#x27;s basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens\">China's basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens</a><ul><li>Part of the positive rights that the social contract guaranteed was economic growth, and the party needed to ensure that the economic elite were on their side in order to ensure that the social contract could be maintained.</li></ul></li></ul>","The around 60% of China’s total trade goes through the Malacca Strait":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Around 60% of China’s total trade and 80% of its crude oil is imported through the Malacca Strait. This means that for China to have strategic access to the strait is extremely important to ensure that they would not be easily isolated from trade if a rival controls the strait.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is crucial for understanding China’s actions in the South China Sea and how they are diversifying the source of their imports.</p><h4>References</h4>","The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The fully realization of the concept of justice arises when it manifests in a city, between free and equal people under the law. Justice may exist elsewhere, but only in a weaker form or in analogy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Aristotle’s unique ontology of justice, something that shapes his ethics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Justice in its most realized sense is political, and regards free and equal persons who share a common life directed towards self-sufficiency under the rule of law. There are two types of political justice:</p><ul><li>Legal justice: arises out of the particular circumstances of the time, which produced unique laws which one must obey.</li><li>Natural justice: arises naturally; the intuitive morality for justice in each individual.</li></ul><p>Domestic/household justice is similar to political justice, since it also specifically regards a community of people. However, the household is hierarchical, with a basis of authority and necessity, not law and reason. Therefore, it is not relevant in a discussion of justice.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>It would seem that when the people of a city lack virtue, that they would then be unable to run a good city? This seems to be echoed later by Thomas Jefferson:</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">“<a href=\"https://www.liberalism.org/p/jefferson-s-liberal-morals?curius=7539&amp;trail=07a868ba\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jefferson’s Liberal Morals</a>”</p><p>Jefferson contended that a virtuous people was more than a nice luxury—calling upon the history of all free societies as his evidence, he thought that <strong>republican government required a certain sort of people to sustain itself.</strong> If the citizenry loses its sense of virtue and starts to become corrupt, they are liable to empower some sort of despotic oligarchy to seize control of the state.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotle's Taxonomy of Justice</span><ul><li>Defines political justice contrastively with other forms of justice.</li></ul></li></ul>","The difference principle requires that expectations be close-knit and chain-connected":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls makes the assumption that there is</p><ul><li>chain-connected expectations, and </li><li>close-knit expectations.</li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>These assumptions help reinforce Rawls’ theories, through they need not actually be the case in order for justice as fairness to be possible.</p><h4>References</h4>","The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The fact that the Japanese were not conquered by the Mongols following two invasion attempts ended the prevalence of outside influence in Japan, as they began to believe that they were superior.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains in part the origins of Japanese isolationism, and their ideology of superiority that would play a part in WWII.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Hojo clan fell due to resentment from other warrior families, the Mongol invasion, and resentment from the emperor\">The Hojo clan fell due to resentment from other warrior families, the Mongol invasion, and resentment from the emperor</a><ul><li>Connected to the Mongol invasion.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take\">Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take</a><ul><li>The Japanese were able to stave off the Mongols because of their unique island geography.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Washington’s Farewell Address inspired American isolationism\">Washington’s Farewell Address inspired American isolationism</a><ul><li>Another case of isolationism, although developing in very different circumstances.</li></ul></li></ul>","The lower the variability around the regression line, the higher the coefficient of determination":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The greater the variability around the least-squares regression line, the lower the $R^2$, meaning that the better the x variable is able to explain the variability in y.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant for understanding the concept of the coefficient of determination, and this value is also important for other fields, such as investing.</p><h4>Graphical Depiction</h4><p> The graph on the left has a higher $R^2$ as there is less variability in the points relative to the right. This also means that the x variable is able to explain more of the variation in the location of the y values.</p><h4>References</h4>","The virtuous man needs friends":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The virtuous man possesses all that is good, and since it is better to have friends than not to have friends, the virtuous man needs friends.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Makes an argument for why the virtuous person needs friends, since, as some say, the virtuous person is already doing well by themselves.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Virtuousness is an activity, and it is easier to be virtuous when you keep up the continuous activity with friends.</li><li>Because virtuous friends is like our second self, when we see others do good things and live well, we, in a meaningful way, see ourselves being the same in our friends.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue\">The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue</a><ul><li>This is the type of friendship referenced in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>Practicing virtue with friends makes this easier.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
